In this lesson we'll look at how strong the evidence really is behind a statistic, because not every impressive result is a reliable one. Once you have a result, the big question is whether the pattern is real, or just sampling noise. Evidence gets stronger when you have a larger random sample, when someone else repeats the study and gets the same answer, when the effect size is big and not tiny, and when the analysis matches the type of data you collected. Evidence gets weaker when the sample is small or biased, when you're relying on a single story or anecdote, when the pattern vanishes on re-measurement, or when everything depends on what people remember and say about themselves. When you write it up, just state the result, how big the effect is, how big and how the sample was taken, and any limitations, and don't claim more than your data can actually support. Now let's see it in action.